    "Catheter ablation is an important treatment for patients with ventricular arrhythmia (VA) when antiarrhythmic medications are ineffective, not tolerated, or not desired. Monomorphic VA usually has an origin or substrate that can be targeted for ablation. Ablation is for selected patients with polymorphic VT/VF only if an initiating PVC focus or substrate can be identified. Ablation strategies, risks, and outcomes depend on the VA's mechanism and location. Most VAs originate close to the subendocardium and are approached transvenously or via transaortic/transseptal catheterization. Some VAs arise from the subepicardium, needing an epicardial approach. Ablation is possible for idiopathic VA and some cardiomyopathies. Scar-related VT is reentry through myocardial fibers with fibrous scarring. Ablation identifies channels within scars associated with slow conduction. VA not tied to structural heart disease or genetic syndromes is often idiopathic and monomorphic. Treatment is via endocardial catheterization, and success is occasionally limited by anesthesia requirements. For ischemic heart disease, patients with sustained VA and SCA survivors are advised for revascularization. Anomalous coronary artery conditions suspected of causing SCA should also prompt repair or revascularization.": []
